Programming a Method
The Method is the container for program routines which you write. To open it and type in the source code
to be executed, double-click its icon in the Frame into which you inserted it. Then, type in your source
code.
Remarks
You can access the functions of the Method 
  on the Edit Ribbon Tab and on the Tools Ribbon Tab.
The figure below shows the following exemplified:
• In the Frame the Tooltip of the Method below the mouse pointer.
• In the Method window the class breakpoint as a filled red circle 
 .
• In the Method window a bookmark as a filled rectangle 
 .
• In the Method window the instance breakpoint which we set to not active, as a white star within a red
circle 
 .
• In the Method window the instance breakpoint as a red star 
 .
The structure of a Method is divided into several parts. If you do not need a certain part, you can delete or
omit it.
A Method which we might, for example, use as an Exit Control, could look like this:
// My Tooltip. My text, my text, my text, ...
param Sender:object
if Sender /= void
Programming a Method
11-3568 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

   @.move(Sender)
   return
end
var MUName:string := @.Name
if MUName = "A"
   @.move(1) // parts named 'A' will be moved to the first successor
else
   @.move(2) // parts named 'B' will be moved to the second successor
end
Plant Simulation highlights the different parts of the syntax of the SimTalk source code in different colors.
Parameters
The structure begins with the declaration of parameters with the keyword param. If you do not need any
parameters, you do not have to declare any.
See also
Parameters [SimTalk]
Declare Parameters
Declare Default Arguments
Programming a Method
Plant Simulation Help 11-3569
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Return Value
Type in the data type of the return value next if the Method is going to return a result (see Declare the
Function Result of a Method).
Local Variables
Declare a local variable by typing in its name and its data type. If you do not need any local variables, you
do not have to declare any.
See also
Local Variables
Declare Local Variables in the Source Code
Data Types in Local Variables
Source Code
Type in the source code which the Method executes. You can type in built-in methods, assignments,
control structures, method calls, branches, and loops. To comment your source code type in two forward
slashes // or two hyphens -- followed by the comment proper.
The Method Editor shows commented source code in a different color than the source code proper.
Note
SimTalk normally does not distinguish between upper- and lower-casing for the names of methods,
attributes, and read-only attributes which you type into the source code of your Method objects.
Note
Click Edit > Reformat to check if you typed the source code for branching and looping correctly.
See also
