Working in the Method Window
Double-click the icon of the Method, which you inserted into a Frame, to open its window. Then type in
your source code, run, and debug it.
Remarks
To change the properties of the Class [general description] of the object, double-click it in the Class
Library or on the tab Information Flow in the Toolbox. The method window provides access to the
Method Editor and to the Method Debugger.
• Click Debug 
  on the Tools ribbon tab or press F11 to switch from the Method Editor to the Method
Debugger.
• Click any of the Step buttons 
  on the debugger toolbar
 repeatedly or once to return to the Method Editor.
• To select a single word, double-click it. To select an entire line of source code, triple-click anywhere in
the line.
• To select all text in the method window, press Ctrl+A or click the mouse button four times.
• To copy the selected text, press Ctrl+C, to paste text from the clipboard, press Ctrl+V.
If you copy source code in the Method Editor, Plant Simulation pastes the copied source code in a
suitable program, for example MS Word, with its color highlighting of the SimTalk syntax.
Plant Simulation places the text, in addition to simple text, as RTF Text, on the clipboard. Suitable
programs paste the formatted RTF text or provide options for the format to be pasted.
Compare this example: We copy the source code of the method MyMethod. Then we paste it with the
paste option Keep Source Formatting into our Word file. When we then copy the source code in Word,
Plant Simulation pastes it with the saved formatting into a new Method.
Working in the Method Window
11-3572 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

If your source code contains Class Breakpoints, Plant Simulation copies these as well when you copy
the source code.
Plant Simulation also applies Class Breakpoints from the class of a Method when you insert an instance
of that Method into your simulation model.
Working in the Method Window
Plant Simulation Help 11-3573
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

If you export the source code of the Method with the command Export to File, you can select the
format of the text file to be exported. To save color highlighting of keywords and comments in the
source code, select HTML Files or RichText Files.
• To move the selected text, drag the mouse to a different location, and drop it.
• To undo the last changes you made to the source code, click 
  or press Ctrl+Z.
• To restore (redo) the source code to the text it had before an undo action, click 
  or press Ctrl+Y.
• To insert a line break, type in a backslash \ if the source code is too long to fit on one line. This makes
it easier to read the source code in the method window while Plant Simulation still interprets the source
code as a single entity.
priority:= "Urgent"
very_long_text := "Frank Jones\
                   Halford Lane 9\
                   Cedar Rapids, IA 52409"
Text := "It is \"very\" urgent." // outputs It is "very" urgent.
• To type in a comment explaining the intention of your source code, type in two forward slashes //
or two hyphens -- at the beginning of the line and then type in the comment. The Method displays
comments in green.
Comments improve the legibility of a Method and help to understand the intentions behind your source
code. A comment is especially helpful in a long Method, when you or a colleague look at it again at a
later point in time to extend its functionality or to debug it. We encourage you to make extensive use of
comments. Comments do not affect the simulation speed at all!
You can insert a comment anywhere, except within a string value of your source code. This means that
you cannot type in a comment within the quotation marks "My string" that designate a string.
◦ To start a one-lined comment, type in two forward slashes // and type in your remarks. This
comment ends at the end of the line.
◦ To start a comment that spans several lines, type in a forward slash and an asterisk /*, and type in
your remarks. To end the comment, type in an asterisk and a forward slash */.
// comment to the end of the line
price := price * 1.16 // value added tax 
/* start of the comment block
   covering several lines
end of the comment block */
• To work with breakpoints in the Method window, use the following key combinations or the respective
context menu commands. The Tools ribbon tab of the Method then activates or deactivates the
respective buttons.
◦ To insert or to delete Class Breakpoints, click in the area of the row markers and breakpoints with the
middle mouse button or the mouse wheel.
Working in the Method Window
11-3574 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

◦ To insert or to delete Instance Breakpoints, hold down Shift and click in the area of the row markers
and breakpoints with the middle mouse button or the mouse wheel.
◦ To activate the breakpoint contained in a line of source code, hold down Ctrl and click the middle
mouse button or the mouse wheel. Instead, you can also press Ctrl+F9.
◦ To open the dialog Breakpoint Settings, press Ctrl+B.
◦ To show a tooltip for a breakpoint, drag the mouse pointer over it and hover.
The tooltip always shows if the respective breakpoint is inactive.
If you configured a Start Time and a Condition, the tooltip shows these as well.
The Method window shows breakpoints like this:
Symbol Description
Designates an active class breakpoint.
Designates a non-active class breakpoint.
Designates an active instance breakpoint.
Designates a non-active instance breakpoint.
Working in the Method Window
Plant Simulation Help 11-3575
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• To scroll horizontally in the Method Editor, hold down Shift and roll the mouse wheel.
• To run the source code of the Method, click Run Method 
  or press F5 or click Debug Method
 or press F11.
• To increase or decrease the font size in the window of the Method Editor or the Method Debugger,
hold down Ctrl and roll the mouse wheel forward and backward.
To decrease the font size, hold down Ctrl and roll the mouse wheel backward. If you change the font
size with the mouse wheel, Plant Simulation uses the changed font size until you change it the next
time or until the end of the Plant Simulation session for all Methods which you open afterward. Plant
Simulation uses the changed font size in all open Method windows, not just in the one in which you
rolled the mouse wheel.
Instead of rolling the mouse wheel, you can also use these key combinations:
Ctrl plus + (plus sign) increases the font size
Ctrl plus 0 resets the font size to the standard size of 10 points
Ctrl plus - (minus sign) decreases the font size
• Select if you want to Show Line Numbers, if you want to highlight keywords and what kind of
indentation you want to use on the Edit Ribbon Tab and on the Tools Ribbon Tab in the active instance
of the Method.
• Select how Plant Simulation shows SimTalk code in all Method objects under File > Preferences > >
Editor in the Plant Simulation window.
An asterisk (*) in the title bar of the Method window shows that the Method contains changes that have
not been applied yet. To do so, click Apply changes 
  on the Edit Ribbon Tab.
The Context Menu of the Method Debugger and the Tools Ribbon Tab also provide a number of
pertaining commands.
